    "editorial:Content_hi.txt": {
      "fingerprint": "32bf10941a9dffc37be9136490858c04f1c1d1aa552d70b42beef9bb679458de",
      "kind": "editorial",
      "status": "blocked",
      "issues": [
        "- Section 4 incorrectly asserts that “सभी A, B हैं” definitely implies “कुछ B, A हैं” (and the Active Recall expects this). In standard syllogism for Indian government exams (SSC, Bank, etc.), universal affirmatives have no existential import, so “Some B are A” does not follow definitely from “All A are B” alone. → Correct the rule: “All A are B” does not guarantee “Some B are A”; the Active Recall should indicate that neither “All B are A” nor “Some B are A” follows definitely without an existence premise."
      ],
